Assistant: Cross-contamination occurs through the transfer of microorganisms from contaminated sources to ready-to-eat foods via intermediate surfaces or direct contact. Research indicates that cutting boards account for approximately 18% of foodborne illness cases linked to home preparation, with wooden boards showing log reductions of 0.5-2.0 for bacteria like Salmonella within 24 hours due to desiccation, while plastic boards can harbor bacteria in knife grooves despite being non-porous. Kitchen sponges represent the highest-risk fomite, with bacterial loads reaching 10^9 CFU/cm³, including pathogenic species in 36% of samples. Transfer rates vary significantly: wet surfaces facilitate 0.1-10% bacterial transfer per contact event, while dry surfaces typically transfer <0.01%. Critical control points include: raw meat juice spreading (can contaminate surfaces up to 50cm away), hand-mediated transfer after handling raw proteins (6-log reduction requires 20-second wash with soap), and utensil reuse between raw and cooked foods. Studies using fluorescent tracers demonstrate that contamination from a single raw chicken breast can spread to 14-17 distinct surfaces during typical meal preparation. Stainless steel and glass surfaces show lower bacterial adhesion rates than plastics, though proper sanitization (>99.9% reduction with 200ppm chlorine solution) remains more important than material selection.

