User: What determines the optimal contact time for different chemical disinfectants in water treatment?
Assistant: Optimal contact time for chemical disinfectants is determined by the CT concept (concentration × time), which represents the product of disinfectant residual concentration and contact time required to achieve specific log reductions of target pathogens. For chlorine, achieving 3-log inactivation of Giardia cysts at pH 7.0 and 10°C requires a CT of approximately 35 mg·min/L, while 4-log virus inactivation requires CT values around 6 mg·min/L. These values vary significantly with temperature (doubling for every 10°C decrease), pH (chlorine effectiveness decreases above pH 7.5 due to HOCl/OCl- equilibrium shifts), and water quality parameters like turbidity and organic content. Chloramine requires CT values 50-100 times higher than free chlorine but provides more stable residual. Ozone demonstrates rapid kinetics with CT values of 0.5-2.0 mg·min/L for most applications, while UV disinfection follows different kinetics based on fluence rather than contact time. The EPA Surface Water Treatment Rule mandates specific CT values based on source water characteristics and treatment goals, with safety factors built in to account for hydraulic inefficiencies and short-circuiting in contact basins.

